19:T55e,## Part-whole trees

A file system is files and folders. A folder contains files **and other folders**, to any depth. A UI is widgets containing widgets. Whenever you have a **tree of part-and-whole**, Composite fits.

## One interface for leaf and branch

Define a **component** interface every node shares. A **leaf** (`File`) implements it directly. A **composite** (`Folder`) implements it too, and **holds a list of components** as children. The crucial move: the composite's children are typed as the *interface*, so a folder can hold files and folders alike.

```
interface FileItem { int size(); }
class File   implements FileItem { int size(){ return bytes; } }
class Folder implements FileItem {
    List<FileItem> children;
    int size(){ return children.stream().mapToInt(FileItem::size).sum(); }  // recurse
}
```

## Uniform treatment is the payoff

The client calls `item.size()` without ever asking *"are you a file or a folder?"*. A folder just sums its children, and each child answers the same way, recursing down the tree. Add, remove, traverse, all uniform.

> Watch the leaf-vs-composite asymmetry: `add()`/`remove()` only make sense on a composite. You either put them on the interface (and have leaves throw) or keep them composite-only, a real design trade-off. Composite is what lets recursion and the **Visitor** pattern walk a structure cleanly.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-structural","activeLesson":"composite"}],["$","$L18","composite",{"lesson":{"id":"composite","patternId":"lld-structural","title":"Composite","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Build a tree of part-and-whole objects, then treat a single leaf and a whole branch THE SAME way through one interface.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Figma"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Leaf and branch, one type","code":["interface FileItem { int size(); }","class File   implements FileItem { ... }","class Folder implements FileItem {","    List<FileItem> children;   // files AND folders","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"FileItem is the shared contract, anything in the tree can report its size().","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"item","name":"FileItem","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ size(): int"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"file","name":"File","fields":["- bytes: int"],"methods":["+ size()"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"folder","name":"Folder","fields":["- children: List"],"methods":["+ size()","+ add(item)"],"x":1.6,"y":1.6,"tint":"accent2"}],"relations":[{"id":"rf","from":"file","to":"item","kind":"implement"},{"id":"ro","from":"folder","to":"item","kind":"implement"},{"id":"rc","from":"folder","to":"item","kind":"aggregate","label":"children","toMult":"*"}],"shownClassIds":["item"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["item"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"File is a LEAF, it implements size() directly (its byte count).","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["item","file"],"shownRelIds":["rf"],"activeIds":["file"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Folder is the COMPOSITE: it implements FileItem too, AND holds a list of FileItems as children, the diamond pointing back at the interface is the self-reference that makes a tree.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["item","file","folder"],"shownRelIds":["rf","ro","rc"],"activeIds":["folder"]},"vars":[["children","List<FileItem>"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"Because children are typed as the interface, a Folder can hold Files and other Folders interchangeably, any depth of tree.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["item","file","folder"],"shownRelIds":["rf","ro","rc"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["holds","leaves AND composites"]]}]},{"label":"Recurse down the tree","code":["int size(){","    return children.stream()","        .mapToInt(FileItem::size)   // each child, leaf or folder","        .sum();","}"],"steps":[{"view":"sequence","codeLine":1,"narration":"The Client asks the root Folder for its size, treating the whole tree as one FileItem.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"root","label":"root : Folder","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"sub","label":"src : Folder","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"f","label":"a.txt : File"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"c","to":"root","label":"size()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"root","to":"f","label":"size()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"f","to":"root","label":"100","kind":"return"},{"id":"m4","from":"root","to":"sub","label":"size()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m5","from":"sub","to":"root","label":"150  (recursed)","kind":"return"},{"id":"m6","from":"root","to":"c","label":"250  (sum)","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0}},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"The Folder asks each child for its size. First child is a File, it just returns its bytes (100).","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["child","File → 100"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"Next child is another Folder. The SAME size() call recurses into it; it sums its own children and returns 150.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":5,"activeIndex":4},"vars":[["child","Folder → 150 (recursed)"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":4,"narration":"The root sums them: 250. The client never branched on type, leaf and branch answered identically, and recursion walked the whole tree. That's Composite.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":6,"activeIndex":5},"vars":[["total","250 ✓"],["client","no type checks ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
